**1. What was failing and why:**
The `dir_len` variable in `search_dir_recursive` was captured before the trailing `/` was appended to the path buffer. After processing the first entry, `pathbuf_truncate(&pb, dir_len)` restored the buffer to just `"t"` (without the slash). On subsequent iterations, appending a filename produced `"tb.txt"` instead of `"t/b.txt"`, so `lstat` failed (ENOENT) and the entry was silently skipped. This also meant subdirectories were never found, and the unreadable-file test never triggered because the file was never reached.

**2. What I changed:**
Moved `size_t dir_len = pb.path_len;` from before the conditional-slash-append to after it, so `dir_len` includes the trailing `/`. Changed the condition to use `pb.path_len` directly.

**3. Build command run:**
`mkdir -p build && cc -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -pedantic -O2 src/new_grep/new_grep.c -o build/new_grep` — passed with no warnings.

**4. Which failing tests I expect to pass now:**
All 9 previously-failing tests should now pass, since they all stem from the same path-construction bug.
